-6a14-5845-a38a-8c057e2a5b8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253372" :question/problem "Given that\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}],$\nfind $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D.$\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries ${\\lambda }_{1},…,{\\lambda }_{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(D)=[\\begin{aligned}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡D & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-253372/field-1" "q-253372/field-2" "q-253372/field-3" "q-253372/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "343f8efc-fdef-51dc-ad3f-fad1ab37bc88"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-253372"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "993ee6db-2930-5b07-8f53-f374df52044c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0fd1bf-523d-5111-800b-c686118e09e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-253261/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-253261/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-2/5c66e59a5ff0682e64ba46f69c126a662b01a651806470ad718db7663ff59f95" :answer/id #uuid "e4e688c3-9a42-59e4-9fa5-27550a951e99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\ln(3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3d7d61-5d83-545f-aaeb-ed25bd9e0f54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-253261/field-2/5c66e59a5ff0682e64ba46f69c126a662b01a651806470ad718db7663ff59f95" :answer-field/choices ["q-253261/field-2/5c66e59a5ff0682e64ba46f69c126a662b01a651806470ad718db7663ff59f95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "c6a8b5f9-b68e-5d57-9bfb-e7ed18530e88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "93095e5a-2bd9-596a-9b04-1aea4bfbb033" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-253261/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-253261/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9a513c4b-115e-5dd1-ac91-7d541cf38ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "3734d401-861b-58d3-831b-da8de0fc8ff9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-253261/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-253261/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253261" :question/id #uuid "3bf189a8-b4c9-5657-942a-45c1a3675468" :question/math-academy-id "q-253261" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n| ${3}^{{J}_{2}(1)}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={3}^{x},$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-253261/field-1" "q-253261/field-2" "q-253261/field-3" "q-253261/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-253261"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-253421/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a73804e8-df44-5cbe-bce5-16fa8621e66b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253421/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "678407d6-fa85-5a4f-917c-bc0472d2f491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/v