field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253877/field-5/a5bcf5044d7131cdf0d721e80125a87ad3d00df1e947910bf38c9dd3889981de" :answer/id #uuid "1f08d61f-c2a6-596f-8daf-16b182ac3874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253877/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2fb75b4-e58e-587f-ad00-df13b014a76c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-253877/field-5/a5bcf5044d7131cdf0d721e80125a87ad3d00df1e947910bf38c9dd3889981de" :answer-field/choices ["q-253877/field-5/a5bcf5044d7131cdf0d721e80125a87ad3d00df1e947910bf38c9dd3889981de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253877" :question/id #uuid "401c9d78-6463-5a6b-ae89-6e5e78fa38e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-253877" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${({J}_{3}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3))}^{4}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $16$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $16$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{3}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3))}^{4} & ={([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{3}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(3)\\end{aligned}])}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{3}(2))}^{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {({J}_{1}(3))}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={({J}_{3}(2))}^{4}\\,⊕\\,{({J}_{1}(3))}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(2),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & 4\\cdot {2}^{3} & \\frac{12\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {2}^{4} & 4\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 32 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{3}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3))}^{4} & ={({J}_{3}(2))}^{4}\\,⊕\\,{({J}_{1}(3))}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 & 24 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-253877/field-1" "q-253877/field-2" "q-253877/field-3" "q-253877/field-4" "q-253877/field-5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-253877"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-253712/field-1/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "f3b9c9bc-fc2d-5a43-afaf-c48f82ec5dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253712/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc2